<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<configuration>

  <packageSources>
    <!--
      VellumText is not on nuget.org. The packages are committed under
      external/vellumtext-packages so a clean clone and a CI runner restore
      exactly what a developer does, with no submodule and no vendored source
      tree. The path is relative on purpose: pointing at the publishing
      machine's folder would restore here and nowhere else.

      Four of the six published packages are here: Core, Avalonia, Interop.Html
      and Interop.Rtf. Interop.Markdown is simply not adopted yet.

      Interop.Json is left out for a reason, and this is the place to read it
      before adding it back.

      Every other importer routes through HtmlImporter and enforces its
      link-scheme allow-list. JsonFormat does not: it reconstructs marks from
      the payload verbatim, so a crafted document keeps javascript:, vbscript:,
      file:/// and protocol-relative //host and \\host targets. That is correct
      for what the format is for - it is the persistence format, and sanitising
      it would destroy a host's own scheme on a round trip - but it means a JSON
      payload must be treated as exactly as trusted as the source that produced
      it. Importing one is equivalent to constructing a DocumentNode directly.

      Nothing here imports VellumText JSON today, and the reference was carried
      in by the 0.4.1 rename rather than adopted for a purpose. It is removed so
      that using it has to be a deliberate act, which is the moment to decide
      whether the payload's source is trusted. If clips are ever stored, synced
      or imported as VellumText JSON, that store is untrusted input in a way a
      pasted HTML fragment is not.
    -->
    <add key="vellumtext-local" value="external/vellumtext-packages" />
  </packageSources>

  <!--
    This mapping is NOT optional, and it is not a supply-chain nicety.

    The library was called Vellum until 0.4.0, and "Vellum.Core" on nuget.org is
    a *different, unrelated* package - a reserved placeholder for an envelope
    encryption library - which publishes the same version numbers, targeting
    net10.0. Pinning a version was therefore no protection at all: restore could
    succeed against the wrong package and the first sign of trouble was a
    compile error naming a type that did not exist. The rename to VellumText.*
    is what actually resolved that, and those ids are unclaimed.

    The mapping stays regardless. It is what makes the local packages
    authoritative for this prefix, so a future squatter on VellumText.* cannot
    silently win a restore, and it is the only thing that would keep working if
    the ids were ever published upstream by someone else.

    packageSourceMapping is all-or-nothing: once any mapping exists, every
    package needs one, or restore fails with NU1100. Hence the "*" entries.

    Several keys are listed for "*" on purpose, because the default public feed
    is not named the same everywhere - a developer machine may reach nuget.org
    through a mirror, while a CI runner uses nuget.org directly. Keys that are
    absent or disabled in a given environment are ignored rather than fatal;
    that was verified, not assumed. Add your own key here if restore reports
    NU1100 for packages that are not VellumText.
  -->
  <packageSourceMapping>
    <packageSource key="vellumtext-local">
      <package pattern="VellumText.*" />
    </packageSource>
    <packageSource key="nuget.org">
      <package pattern="*" />
    </packageSource>
    <packageSource key="azure-default">
      <package pattern="*" />
    </packageSource>
    <packageSource key="Microsoft Visual Studio Offline Packages">
      <package pattern="*" />
    </packageSource>
  </packageSourceMapping>

</configuration>
